WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.821
You have seen this in games. A shiny metal pipe in the distance, and its highlight fizzes and sparkles.

2
00:00:07.171 --> 00:00:13.187
Move the camera, and the sparkle crawls across the surface, like static on an old television.

3
00:00:13.537 --> 00:00:22.647
Here is that effect on a steel roller. The left half of the highlight is smooth. Further right, the metal gets rougher, and the highlight breaks into glitter.

4
00:00:22.997 --> 00:00:29.845
Same roller, same lamp, same texture. One small formula turns the glitter into a soft, wider glow.

5
00:00:30.195 --> 00:00:34.013
That formula is the Toksvig factor. Let's see why it works.

6
00:00:35.213 --> 00:00:40.355
Here is the puzzle. Zoom into the metal, and its surface is covered in tiny bumps.

7
00:00:40.705 --> 00:00:47.915
Each bump tilts the surface a little. The direction a surface faces is called its normal, drawn here as an arrow.

8
00:00:48.265 --> 00:00:55.561
But from far away, a single pixel covers a whole patch of these bumps. Here, sixteen by sixteen texels.

9
00:00:55.911 --> 00:01:04.743
If the pixel looks at just one normal, the one in the centre, it gets a random answer. Either it catches the lamp, or it misses completely.

10
00:01:05.093 --> 00:01:11.643
Neighbouring pixels pick different bumps, so the highlight flickers between bright and dark. That is the sparkle.

11
00:01:12.843 --> 00:01:16.427
The obvious fix is to average all the normals in the patch.

12
00:01:16.777 --> 00:01:23.433
Every normal has length one. But when they point in different directions, their average comes out shorter than one.

13
00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:30.524
Call that length r. If all the bumps agree, r is one. The more they disagree, the shorter r gets.

14
00:01:30.874 --> 00:01:34.458
On our hero patch, r is zero point nine eight six.

15
00:01:34.808 --> 00:01:40.952
That looks almost like one. But the highlight is so sharp that even this small shortening matters a lot.

16
00:01:42.152 --> 00:01:52.029
How sharp is the highlight? We use a simple shading model. Take the cosine of the angle between the normal and the half vector, and raise it to a power s.

17
00:01:52.379 --> 00:01:59.547
The half vector is the direction halfway between the eye and the lamp. When the normal lines up with it, we see the highlight.

18
00:01:59.897 --> 00:02:07.215
The exponent s controls the width. A big exponent gives a tiny, sharp highlight. Here, s is sixty-four.

19
00:02:07.565 --> 00:02:16.439
Now here is the key fact. Averaging many sharp highlights, each from a slightly tilted bump, gives one blurrier highlight, lower and wider.

20
00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:20.477
So what should the pixel do with the average normal?

21
00:02:20.827 --> 00:02:25.861
Option one. Stretch it back to length one, and keep the exponent at sixty-four.

22
00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:32.739
The sparkle is gone, but the highlight is still the narrow, polished one. It ignores how rough the metal is.

23
00:02:33.089 --> 00:02:36.801
Option two. Keep the short normal, and keep the exponent.

24
00:02:37.151 --> 00:02:44.469
The peak gets dimmer, but the width does not change at all. Both curves are half as bright at eight point four two degrees.

25
00:02:44.819 --> 00:02:49.597
Dimming is not blurring. To widen the highlight, we need to lower the exponent.

26
00:02:50.797 --> 00:02:55.747
That is exactly what Toksvig does. He turns the length r into a factor f.

27
00:02:56.097 --> 00:03:00.683
f equals r, divided by r plus s times one minus r.

28
00:03:01.033 --> 00:03:04.169
Then the new exponent, s prime, is f times s.

29
00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:09.277
When the normals all agree, r is one, so f is one, and nothing changes.

30
00:03:09.627 --> 00:03:20.443
On the hero patch, r is zero point nine eight six, and the factor is zero point five two eight. The exponent drops from sixty-four to about thirty-four.

31
00:03:20.793 --> 00:03:28.622
And here is the aha. The shortening you can barely see in the arrow is precisely the information that the highlight should be blurrier.

32
00:03:29.822 --> 00:03:36.115
There is another way to read r. Define alpha squared as one minus r, divided by r.

33
00:03:36.465 --> 00:03:42.780
It acts like the variance of the bumps. It is zero for a mirror, and it grows as the slopes spread out.

34
00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:49.445
With it, the factor becomes even simpler. f equals one, over one plus s times alpha squared.

35
00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:58.712
So the exponent is lowered by exactly how much the normals vary. On the hero patch, alpha squared is zero point zero one four.

36
00:03:59.912 --> 00:04:05.480
Now the measurement. These curves show highlight brightness against angle, for the hero patch.

37
00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:12.806
The white curve is the truth. We shade every single texel at exponent sixty-four, and average the results.

38
00:04:13.156 --> 00:04:22.927
The red curve is the renormalized normal. It peaks at one, almost twice too bright, and it is half as bright at only eight point four two degrees.

39
00:04:23.277 --> 00:04:31.149
The orange curve is Toksvig. Its peak is zero point five three five, against the true zero point five two four.

40
00:04:31.499 --> 00:04:40.267
And it falls to half at eleven point five seven degrees, against the true eleven point six four. It sits right on top of the truth.

41
00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:45.648
Across the whole roller, from polished to blasted, the picture holds.

42
00:04:45.998 --> 00:04:53.550
Single normal shading flickers. Its brightness jumps around with a standard deviation of zero point two seven eight.

43
00:04:53.900 --> 00:05:00.471
Toksvig stays steady at zero point zero one five. That is about eighteen and a half times calmer.

44
00:05:00.821 --> 00:05:06.879
And against the true average, Toksvig's error is only about two percent of the renormalized error.

45
00:05:07.229 --> 00:05:15.656
The rougher the land, the more the factor drops, and the wider the Toksvig band becomes, exactly as the averaged truth says it should.

46
00:05:16.856 --> 00:05:25.069
Now the honest part. This is one slope texture, one sixteen by sixteen box, and a software renderer on the C P U.

47
00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:32.779
It does not prove anything about real G P U mipmaps, stretched footprints, or brushed metal with a direction.

48
00:05:33.129 --> 00:05:44.265
And one check was nudged. The sparkle threshold was lowered to zero point one four nine, because the gentlest rough land measured zero point one four nine five.

49
00:05:44.615 --> 00:05:50.141
That does not change the ranking. The run finishes with fourteen checks passed and none failed.

50
00:05:51.341 --> 00:05:52.685
So, the takeaway.

51
00:05:53.035 --> 00:05:59.691
The average normal gets shorter. The exponent drops. The highlight widens. And the sparkle disappears.

52
00:06:00.041 --> 00:06:10.473
This idea, often called specular anti aliasing, is a standard trick in real time rendering, and you can find its descendants in modern game engines.

53
00:06:10.823 --> 00:06:17.415
The original is Michael Toksvig, Mipmapping Normal Maps, from two thousand five. Thanks for watching.
